[38;5;231m⏺[39m Scope: both. Next fork — and this is the one that determines whether this is a small module or a real subsystem.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a log entry has to leave the machine that wrote it. In the browser, [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m in production is
  invisible to you — the log dies in a user's devtools you'll never see. So "debug production issues" either means
  "structured output that a user or your own Node process can hand you," or it means shipping logs to a collector.

  - [1mConsole only (structured + levelled)[0m — levels, timestamps, JSON-ish structured fields, one API for both halves. Zero
    dependencies, nothing to operate. Buys you readable Node logs and browser logs a user can copy out of devtools.
    Rules out passive browser diagnostics: if you can't reproduce it, you never see it.
  - [1mConsole now, transport seam built in[0m — same module, but sinks are an interface and console is the only
    implementation today. Adding a remote sink later is a config change, not an edit to every call site. Cheap now;
    retrofitting the seam across call sites later is the expensive version.
  - [1mShip to a collector now[0m — batching, retry/backoff, page-unload flush, consent, and PII redaction all become
    load-bearing immediately. It also needs a real endpoint: [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m here points at [38;5;153mapi.example.com[39m, a stub, so
    there's nothing to send to yet.

  Sink choice is cheap to change later [3mif[0m the seam exists; call-site changes are the part that's expensive to redo.
  That's the asymmetry.

  [1mMy recommendation: console now with a transport seam.[0m It matches what this repo can actually support today — there's
  no collector to send to — while making the remote sink a later config change rather than a rewrite.
